  "curriculum": "Utrecht University identifies Clinical Sciences as the English name of the Dutch-taught Zorg, gezondheid en samenleving bachelor. The dated OER, articles 3.5–3.7, defines 180 EC and a minimum of 45 EC at advanced level. The first five semesters account for 150 EC. The first course is 15 EC, followed by eighteen 7.5 EC courses. The final 30 EC is 15 EC of elective courses and a 15 EC Societal Challenge. The student selects a topic within the degree. The regulations allow eligible approved choices without excessive overlap. The degree integrates veterinary, pharmaceutical and medical perspectives, with professional practice and portfolio-based assessment.",
  "progression": "The first year progresses from early development and molecular systems to anatomy and physiology across species and introductory mechanisms of health and disease, alongside care systems, philosophy and practice exploration. Second-year classes add infection, organ-system disease, quantitative research, therapeutics, health systems and access, technology and mental health. Third-year core study applies this foundation to biotechnology, practice dilemmas, healthy environments and ageing. The normal plan completes all 60 EC in year one and all 60 in year two before the Societal Challenge, exceeding the article 4.2 entry threshold of all year-one credits and 30 EC of year-two work. The third year supplies 30 EC of fixed advanced courses and 15 EC of advanced final work. The student guide identifies the final teaching block for the Societal Challenge.",
